  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 303 PM EDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\n- Showers and storms moving to the South; Patchy fog overnight\n\n The quasistationary boundary continues to sag southward and will \npush any showers/storms in that direction. There has been some\nlight rain along the I 94 corridor, with the anvil from the\nthunderstorms through southern Michigan bringing some rumbles of\nthunder and vicinity lighting to that region. However, the storms\nthemselves continue their southward trek toward the southeast. \n Light winds, a moist surface layer and recent rainfall is a\nrecipe for patchy fog overnight into Wednesday morning. Expect\nthis fog to be dense at times and will linger through the morning\ncommute. \n\n- Chances for storms tomorrow\n\nThough there is a signal for decent CAPE values from the SFC \nmoisture and daytime temperatures into the 80s, the atmosphere is \nfairly stable, especially since the boundary remains to the south.\nLatest HREF does show the potential for a weak mid level trough \nand some shear through northern lower Michigan in the afternoon. \nThat could take advantage of the 1K J/kg of CAPE Wednesday \nafternoon. For those reasons, SPC has a ribbon of marginal risk of\nsevere storms through the central and northern half of Lower MI \ntomorrow afternoon and evening. \n\n\n- Next chance for Showers/storms over the weekend\n\nThe large upper level low over northern Canada continues to send\nwaves through Michigan through the week. However, we remain \nfairly stable through the latter half of the week with \ntemperatures at, to below normal into the weekend. A large upper \nlevel low will move into the the Great Lakes region through the \narea mid to late weekend with chance for rain into Sunday.",
